    "slug": "triad-diminished-across-the-sets",
    "title": "Twelve Grips, and Two That Can't Come Home",
    "summary": "Every diminished grip is the minor grip with the fifth dropped a fret, except two that have to wait for the next octave — and the reason the shape never repeats itself.",
    "tags": ["triads", "chords", "diminished", "fretboard"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-14"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The recipe for every diminished grip is the one that built "
        },
        {
          "text": "Cdim",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " at the start of this chapter",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "triad-the-flat-fifth"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": ": take the minor grip in the same place and drop the fifth another fret. The inversion says which finger moves — top note in root position, middle note in first inversion, bottom note in second. Ten of the twelve grips on the neck work exactly that way."
        }
      ]
    },
