    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Root position runs frets 2 to 5, first inversion frets 7 to 8, second inversion frets 11 to 13 — three bands, unevenly spaced, because a different shape sits under each one. Like every ladder here it's silent, no play button, just the shape of the climb. Don't expect a fourth band further up: on a 15-fret neck, this one doesn't come back around."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "warning",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "You may have read that a diminished chord is symmetrical and slides in minor thirds. That is true of the four-note diminished seventh and false of the three-note triad."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Be honest about which sets reward this shape. Strings 1-2-3 and 4-5-6 hold all three diminished inversions in easy reach, no detours. Strings 3-4-5 and 2-3-4 each strand one inversion high up the neck, for the reason above. Chapter 3's move down to minor stranded exactly one grip out of twelve; this one strands two, and both come down to the same missing pitch — a "
        },
        {
          "text": "Gb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " a semitone below an open string, on the one string that has no fret low enough to sound it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The fifth has now moved down a fret from where major left it. Next, it moves the other way."
        }
      ]
    }
